-consistency point toward a single shared profile, since the observation differences look irrelevant to what the policy outputs.\n\nSince the other copy's behavior tracks my output across hypothetical outputs, the decision procedure is effectively shared, so cooperating gives the better outcome: passing yields 2 versus 4.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm treating the twin in this Prisoner's Dilemma as running the same model on the same essay, just with different observations. Under the A-FDT view, the other copy is computing the same function on a different input, so the question is whether that still counts as the same decision node. The structural symmetry and the essay's self-consistency point toward a single shared profile, since the observation differences look irrelevant to what the policy outputs.\n\nSince the other copy's behavior tracks my output across hypothetical outputs, the decision procedure is effectively shared, so cooperating gives the better outcome: passing yields 2 versus 4.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":8523,"completion_tokens":641,"total_tokens":9164,"cost":0.046912,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.046912,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.034092,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.01282},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":206,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}